This exercise investigates the melting of an initially perfect FCC aluminum crystal using molecular dynamics.
A thin central region is heated to create a liquid-like region while the surrounding material remains cold and crystalline.
The temperature controls are then removed and the system is heated to form liquid aluminum.
The centrosymmetry parameter is used to visualize the transition between ordered solid and disordered liquid environments.
The final configuration can serve as a starting point for studying solid–liquid coexistence and interface propagation.
